## CLI-facing: yes, and exercised as a CLI

This bead ships a new command, so the surface was driven from a shell, not only
through tests: `--help`, every required-flag omission, all three error paths, and
two complete live gate runs against real seats. Both live runs are recorded on
`refs/etude/runs/etude-9uf.1` and pushed to origin.

## The merge with `origin/main`

The branch was rebased onto `origin/main`, where another lane had landed
`invocation_fallbacks` plus a guard asserting the checked-in registry equals
`registry.Default()`. Both designs were kept: fallbacks are the config-level
candidate list, the adapter is the execution-level bridge. Their guard did its
job — it caught that the YAML edits had not been mirrored into `Default()`.

Their two tests were updated rather than bypassed: the golden registry YAML was
regenerated from `Default()`, and `TestSeatInvocationsReturnsIndependentSlice`
now captures the real first fallback instead of hardcoding `"agy"` (which is now
the sub-agent). That test is about aliasing, not ordering, so binding it to a
literal name made it fail on an unrelated change; it is stronger now.

## Seats

The opus seat runs as an in-harness sub-agent — a fresh, separately
authenticated opus identity given only the shared gate prompt and the inlined
artifact. That is the arrangement `.etude/registry.yaml` documents, and it is now
machine-honored as the first `invocation_fallbacks` entry rather than living in a
comment a worker could read and stop at. Its verdict is a normal seat verdict and
its record carries no `degraded_reason`.

## Known limitation, disclosed

`etude gate` resolves `invoke` and execs it. It does not yet ACT on
`invocation_fallbacks`: when the primary invocation fails it records the seat
`failed` rather than trying the next candidate. The config now expresses the
ladder faithfully and `Seat.Invocations()` returns it in order, so the consumer
side is a contained follow-up rather than a redesign. Filed as **etude-5f6**.

## Gate outcome

The implement gate PASSED at round 8 — codex GO, opus GO — on a tree frozen and
sha256-verified across both verdicts. Eight rounds; six of them caught a real
defect. Every BLOCK was in my work, and none was argued down.

Rounds 5, 6 and 7 turned out to be ONE family: a parsing rule that is safe in one
direction being unsafe in the other. r5 and r6 were fail-OPEN (a stated BLOCK, and
then a cancelled finding, each becoming a pass). r7 was fail-CLOSED — my r6 fix
removed an implicit tolerance the buggy rule had been providing, so an echoing
seat could no longer cast a GO at all. A false outage is not the safe failure it
looks like: it escalates every gate rather than one seat.

Round 8's four non-blocking follow-ups were all folded before commit, with
regressions, and confirmed by the seat that raised them. Two were further
fail-opens: a label with a space before its colon (`BLOCKING : 1. real finding`)
read as an empty body, and the placeholder rule was body-shaped so a genuine
angle-wrapped finding was dropped. The fourth added the `anchorCommand` unit test
that the round-6 fix had shipped without; a negative control proves it fails if
that bug is reintroduced.

## What the opus seat found once it was convened properly

The seat's first real verdict on this bead was a BLOCK, and it was the most
valuable finding of the epic. `scripts/seat-adapter.sh` applied "last VERDICT
line wins" even when an EARLIER line-anchored VERDICT resolved to the opposite
valid token, so a reply of the form

    VERDICT: BLOCK
    BLOCKING: 1. real defect
    ...
    If fixed I would say:
    VERDICT: GO

exited 0 and wrote `{"verdict":"go","required":["1. real defect"]}`. Nothing
downstream compensates — `synthesizeVerdict` counts the token and discards
`required` — so a stated BLOCK became a passing vote. The seat verified it
against the real script; I reproduced it before changing anything.

Position no longer arbitrates between two valid verdicts, in either direction. A
second opus instance reviewing the same packet independently named that exact
attack as its best one and recommended the identical hardening, which is why this
was treated as certain rather than arguable.

Five smaller findings from the same pair of reviews were folded with regressions:
a `GO` carrying blocking findings is refused (raised by both seats); the prompt is
not guessed when several inputs exist; the envelope pre-clear is verified; `trap`
covers `INT`/`TERM`; and `anchorSeatCommands` anchors fallback commands too. Two
residual items are filed as **etude-vnf** (P3) rather than fixed.

## Correction: the opus seat was always available

Earlier in this epic I reported the in-harness opus seat as unreachable after six
attempts, and filed `etude-zc3` on that basis. That diagnosis was wrong in an
important way, and the cause was mine.

Every one of those six spawns gave the agent a NAME. A named agent becomes a
persistent, addressable teammate: it runs, goes idle, and waits to be messaged —
it never returns a report to the caller. An unnamed spawn launches async and
delivers its result through a completion notification, which is the shape a
one-shot reviewer seat needs.

So the seat was reachable the whole time; I was invoking it in a shape that
cannot return a verdict, and then reading the resulting silence as an outage. The
`agy` substitute I fell back on was never needed, and the quota exhaustion that
followed was a consequence of that wrong turn rather than a cause of anything.

`etude-zc3` is updated accordingly rather than closed silently, because the
misdiagnosis is the useful part: a seat that "returns nothing" should prompt a
check of the invocation shape before it is recorded as an outage.
